I. Introduction

As part of the Electron-Ion Collider (EIC) a new collider currently being built at Brookhaven National Laboratory (BNL) colliding beams of electrons and ions. This is realized as a collaborative effort between BNL and Jefferson Lab (JLab) with planned start of operations in the early 2030s. The EIC will be a discovery machine for unlocking the secrets of the “glue” that binds the building blocks of visible matter in the universe [1]. The EIC General Purpose Detector system, ePIC, is located at Interaction Point 6 (IP6) and includes a 2T detector solenoid (MARCO) at its heart with an operational central field of 2 T. The MARCO solenoid is a large-sized superconducting magnet [2]. Since the magnet is a critical long-lead item and the core of the detector, the conductor is provided to the magnet vendor at no cost. The conductor design is finalized in advance and is already in production with the conductor vendor to mitigate any schedule risks.

